[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-9457"] :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3} & =1 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-4\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =-5,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-5,0)$ and $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/f491b886477531fbd081cbdd73244e9cf489bd2474528ea27b66b187b9eb170e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection/b965dcc7056d863fed9108a3b869af1bd627e6481b751e0c3e89ded08ede951c" :answer/id #uuid "d8635ba2-c02d-5b70-adf7-4d27026af194" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" :answer/id #uuid "c4d2ee77-b7e7-5264-8fa6-a323a3d65daf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection/21222b9c4748093197e8e6a8fabbba14cf9b24871c4c9556fbb1213d9d4bcb0a" :answer/id #uuid "e6b7ca58-6523-5b4a-89aa-333c3f91d9ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection/9605fb6e3a6ac67fff837fcbd370d249aec778e15ce5f7f05e7b85a146588a74" :answer/id #uuid "1cb02340-833a-56ca-8205-4cd7a9dbc4e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection/4091bfd76ae298ceb4a683cffe12a124fd2f166152e89ed75cbf45912771acf2" :answer/id #uuid "e283c48b-6c89-599d-b881-478cdb455ee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "64d96e1d-dc55-5d53-92ab-ea6ad3148922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-16647/selection/9605fb6e3a6ac67fff837fcbd370d249aec778e15ce5f7f05e7b85a146588a74" :answer-field/choices ["q-16647/selection/b965dcc7056d863fed9108a3b869af1bd627e6481b751e0c3e89ded08ede951c" "q-16647/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" "q-16647/selection/21222b9c4748093197e8e6a8fabbba14cf9b24871c4c9556fbb1213d9d4bcb0a" "q-16647/selection/9605fb6e3a6ac67fff837fcbd370d249aec778e15ce5f7f05e7b85a146588a74" "q-16647/selection/4091bfd76ae298ceb4a683cffe12a124fd2f166152e89ed75cbf45912771acf2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16647" :question/id #uuid "69c2f5cb-5a68-5c5e-91e4-a196dc268883" :question/math-academy-id "q-16647" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2}=72$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-3)^{2}+9{y}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ 4(x-3)^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm \\sqrt{18} \\\\ (x-3) & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(3-3\\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(3+3\\sqrt{2},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-325860/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "fa95007f-6fd9-5756-801b-816fb1df33c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325860/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f4458648-e014-54c8-815f-21e40d8b9198" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325860/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-325860/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325860/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "930ecd69-e12f-50bf-b093-e7aeb4035088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-325860/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b2c65e-e8f1-5ef1-83b8-6dfe7c5bedce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-325860/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-325860/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-325860" :question/id #uuid "df2710fa-49d8-5662-a9c3-1c15d9f9c3a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325860" :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis.\n\nThe $x$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(0-6)^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{36}{36} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9}+1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{9} & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958862/assets/5a1377fa0ff46ff7a7e4b685d38852ad67abd8c0adfc432fc22d7c1d57b7a57f.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-325860/field-1" "q-325860/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-325860"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection/5e165a9c1051c8bd0d1d464cf8bd1ca416c4b5ae4fd5069c7e677981a165cb22" :answer/id #uuid "7538da33-4499-5a56-8ee1-78740b96168b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection/85d1076c5b74bf21ed8972dee401d0e5408cbc13be734408a1a69cf8a1d9de75" :answer/id #uuid "f715465a-681c-5546-aaf4-b72dcccc54c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection/94b3c4c50449910b51bd3f5f063c5e664102770f23449d48044e26c0119351c1" :answer/id #uuid "ef1a01ae-c095-5d05-8219-c89f6c6f83db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" :answer/id #uuid "dcf660b4-d4f5-5e3c-bbc1-0920eaadb2d9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection/d0e36a67ce2b72e5b5400b15157cfed8d092b47a4d750569319322ba19dca5d9" :answer/id #uuid "8d804631-6000-5274-9589-897a4691f407" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "06d0653b-a7e0-51d3-b91c-7d5c19f29317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-16642/selection/85d1076c5b74bf21ed8972dee401d0e5408cbc13be734408a1a69cf8a1d9de75" :answer-field/choices ["q-16642/selection/5e165a9c1051c8bd0d1d464cf8bd1ca416c4b5ae4fd5069c7e677981a165cb22" "q-16642/selection/85d1076c5b74bf21ed8972dee401d0e5408cbc13be734408a1a69cf8a1d9de75" "q-16642/selection/94b3c4c50449910b51bd3f5f063c5e664102770f23449d48044e26c0119351c1" "q-16642/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" "q-16642/selection/d0e36a67ce2b72e5b5400b15157cfed8d092b47a4d750569319322ba19dca5d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16642" :question/id #uuid "fca3c3f7-188d-5506-9818-be66c0607803" :question/math-academy-id "q-16642" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ 9(x-3)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(3-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0)$ and $(3+\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16642/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16642"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection/368733aa343e96447c677797b459d5a5ffd932f886bfd37a5bc9165a665aa584" :answer/id #uuid "01ad77f8-16b1-5bb8-ad29-59b9ef0fc86e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" :answer/id #uuid "e56e4c0e-b228-5a52-8826-1f64bc0761ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection/ed63d48d82acb9f420f517e546e18ee53ac269c10cb34faeac60d635cfd2dbb5" :answer/id #uuid "26a1c010-0e1b-5e5b-bc36-75a8df257575" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm 1,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection/3b00ab363ae2f8a8b3770e91169974428b106c03488291be0f865a049826b81e" :answer/id #uuid "bfb48277-c593-5ee0-8b65-8b6011b2eb4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection/cf46b811fa2a970a1a2655ca84b0c7190104b2c2da0fd0b2c8486eb3e7d46107" :answer/id #uuid "d7b23ee3-7a6f-51f4-98c2-c0dccec79917" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(1,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b32eca97-6dd3-5ee1-a315-2b1fcbdb1bb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-16646/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" :answer-field/choices ["q-16646/selection/368733aa343e96447c677797b459d5a5ffd932f886bfd37a5bc9165a665aa584" "q-16646/selection/3bd45debee7b0d8601f1f10f0ea3890d1173d903090c37b4d5442f224a5ae970" "q-16646/selection/ed63d48d82acb9f420f517e546e18ee53ac269c10cb34faeac60d635cfd2dbb5" "q-16646/selection/3b00ab363ae2f8a8b3770e91169974428b106c03488291be0f865a049826b81e" "q-16646/selection/cf46b811fa2a970a1a2655ca84b0c7190104b2c2da0fd0b2c8486eb3e7d46107"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-16646" :question/id #uuid "50559ddf-048e-5af7-bb48-9f6c1a546726" :question/math-academy-id "q-16646" :question/problem "Calculate the points if any, where the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2}+20=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2(y-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(0-4)^{2} & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2(16) & =-20 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+32 & =-20 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{52}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{52}{5}.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-16646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-16646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection/fc85d2eb467fca068ae74320a67da8c7234c47bfe7236694e3e42837a7ea6836" :answer/id #uuid "fc414fcd-71aa-5c5a-868f-9550b1919c90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(3,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection/368733aa343e96447c677797b459d5a5ffd932f886bfd37a5bc9165a665aa584" :answer/id #uuid "c4eb3833-664e-5674-95bb-5ad675bd5959" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm 4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection/21222b9c4748093197e8e6a8fabbba14cf9b24871c4c9556fbb1213d9d4bcb0a" :answer/id #uuid "ad101bbd-2947-5f49-aece-d36f4d805851" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\pm 3,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection/90aa502b2da56240345276577d45ffbbdb86f6952f2a76a2caaa59a3c1464199" :answer/id #uuid "3f01f6b0-8922-5a07-9fde-22aa4b0105b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(7,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection/b8296888bdf63be9041b4c8218880b5c6769cde1d622b7075bd5fa5bbde2d13a" :answer/id #uuid "0221d733-e017-502e-a01a-2b1025b7e60f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(4,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3ca1a7-a59a-52f0-8e90-2a7cb91cb05e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170548/selection/90aa502b2da56240345276577d45ffbbdb86f6952f2a76a2caaa59a3c1464199" :answer-field/choices ["q-170548/selection/fc85d2eb467fca068ae74320a67da8c7234c47bfe7236694e3e42837a7ea6836" "q-170548/selection/368733aa343e96447c677797b459d5a5ffd932f886bfd37a5bc9165a665aa584" "q-170548/selection/21222b9c4748093197e8e6a8fabbba14cf9b24871c4c9556fbb1213d9d4bcb0a" "q-170548/selection/90aa502b2da56240345276577d45ffbbdb86f6952f2a76a2caaa59a3c1464199" "q-170548/selection/b8296888bdf63be9041b4c8218880b5c6769cde1d622b7075bd5fa5bbde2d13a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170548" :question/id #uuid "826b5f6d-dee6-5776-af58-0c5d7c0d4784" :question/math-academy-id "q-170548" :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y=7$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-6x+12y & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(0)^{2}-6x+12(0) & =7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =7 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)-9 & =7 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =-1,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-1,0)$ and $(7,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-170548/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6381faa4-5dfd-58fa-ace7-411fd2a97e98"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-274483/field-1/88ae2ed83a124270ebee6014bf7a9d64ef5b7a5fdae5272b6dee62df64c21aac" :answer/id #uuid "eb381392-dd0c-5031-a6d4-51dd84f7d05f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :an